## Why the general module is no obstruction (corrects FINDING 3)

The paper's induction step reads `A = A₀ ⊕ μ_p`, which looks like a restriction to direct sums of
one-dimensional modules.  It is not: **every clause of the `z`-data lives over `G_K`, where the
action of `Gal(K|k)` is trivial**, so no equivariance of the decomposition is needed and one may
split `E` along an arbitrary *linear* hyperplane.  Concretely, `Work.SSGRoute.IsZData.map` transports
the `z`-data along any `𝔽_p`-linear map and `Work.SSGRoute.IsZData.split` performs the paper's
verification for `E = ker ρ ⊕ ⟨z₀⟩`; iterating over `dim_{𝔽_p} E` (`z_data_general_of_mup_and_aux`,
strong induction on the finrank) gives *every* finite coefficient module.  In particular no general
local Tate pairing, no self-duality `A ≅ A^∨(1)` and no retraction argument is needed — the general
case is *not* harder than the `μ_p` case beyond the realisation input.

