"`ytilde` is unramified at every finite place outside `S₀`".  That is *false* for the datum of a
class `ν` satisfying the hypotheses of `Work.SSGRoute.IsRealisationInput`; it has been replaced by
"unramified at the places outside `S₀` that are not split in the auxiliary field `Λ`" — the new
*parameter* of the predicate (`Λ`), which is exactly the shape in which the one-dimensional statement
at the auxiliary pair `(Λ, S₀)` consumes it (`IsYRealisation.zStatementHypotheses`).

The refutation of the old form is elementary and complete.  Take `k = K = ℚ`, `p = 2`,
`Λ = ℚ(√5)`, `T = S₀ = {2, 5, 13}` and let `ν = χ₄₁` be the quadratic class of `41`.  Then `ν` is a
continuous homomorphism (so additive and locally constant on `G_ℚ`); it is unramified at the places
of `S₀` (`41 ≡ 1 mod 8`, `41 ≡ 1 mod 5`, `13 ∤ 41`); it is unramified at every place outside `S₀`
that is not split in `Λ` (it is ramified only at `41`, and `41 ≡ 1 mod 5`, so `41` splits in
`ℚ(√5)`); it vanishes on the whole local group of the place `2 ∈ S₀`, which lies above `p = 2`
(`41 ≡ 1 mod 8` says `41 ∈ (ℚ₂^×)²`, so the local component at `2` is the trivial class); and it
vanishes on the involution `c` (`41 > 0` means `c` acts trivially).  So `ν` satisfies every
hypothesis.  A realisation `ỹ` satisfying the *old* clause 5 would be an element of
`Hom(G_ℚ, μ₂) = ℚ^×/ℚ^{×2}` whose local components are trivial at `2` and at `5`, equal to the
